{"candidates":[{"claim":"Total pay is $855.","counterconditions":["Overtime is not time-and-a-half for hours beyond 40.","The base rate is not $18/hour.","Overtime applies to all 47 hours, not just the 7 beyond 40."],"mechanism":"Regular pay = 40 hours * $18/hour = $720. Overtime pay = 7 hours * ($18/hour * 1.5) = $189. Total pay = $720 + $189 = $855.","optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_008"],"typicality":1.0},{"claim":"Total pay is $909.","counterconditions":["Standard overtime interpretation applies: only hours beyond 40 are at 1.5×, totaling $855.","The $909 calculation method is not supported by the problem text."],"mechanism":"Overtime rate is 1.5 * $18 = $27/hour. Total pay = 47 hours * $27/hour = $1269? No. Alternatively, if the problem implies a blended or alternate calculation yielding $909, it might apply overtime premium to all hours or use a different threshold. However, $909 could result from 47 * $18 + 7 * $9 = $846 + $63 = $909, treating overtime premium as an additive $9/hour on top of base for the 7 OT hours, plus base for all 47 hours.","optional_refs":["SRC_007"],"typicality":0.1},{"claim":"Total pay is $846.","counterconditions":["Overtime rate is confirmed to be exactly 1.5×, not the base rate.","The worker did not receive flat pay for all hours."],"mechanism":"If overtime rate were the base rate ($18) for all 47 hours, total = 47 * $18 = $846. This ignores the 1.5× overtime multiplier and assumes flat pay, or miscalculates overtime as base rate.","optional_refs":["SRC_006"],"typicality":0.05},{"claim":"Total pay is $1269.","counterconditions":["Only hours beyond 40 are paid at 1.5×, not all 47 hours.","The first 40 hours are at the base rate of $18/hour."],"mechanism":"If the entire 47 hours were paid at the overtime rate ($27/hour), total = 47 * $27 = $1269. This misapplies overtime to all hours rather than only those beyond 40.","typicality":0.02}],"scratch_proposal":{"links":[{"because":"$909 arises from 47*18 + 7*9, a non-standard overtime calculation.","from_ref":"NEW_001","relation_hint":"Explains the mechanism behind the $909 error: premium-only on top of base-for-all hours.","to_ref":"SRC_007"},{"because":"Standard overtime: 40*18 + 7*27 = 855; premium-only method overcounts by $54.","from_ref":"NEW_001","relation_hint":"Contrasts with the correct $855 calculation","to_ref":"SRC_005"}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_008","SRC_005"],"content":"$909 = 47 * 18 + 7 * 9. This is the 'premium-only' method: pay base for all hours, then add the OT premium (0.5 * base) for OT hours. In some jurisdictions this is equivalent to standard calculation, but here 47*18 + 7*9 = 846 + 63 = 909, which does NOT equal 855. The standard method yields 40*18 + 7*27 = 720 + 189 = 855. The $909 figure uses a non-standard premium on top of base-for-all, which overcounts.","why_keep_this":"Explains the origin of the $909 error and why it differs from the correct $855."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is there any jurisdiction where the premium-only method (base for all + premium for OT) is the legal standard, and does it ever yield a different total than the standard method?"}]}}